Complexity of Approach 1

Suppose there are n nodes.

BFS visits every node:

O(n)

Collections.reverse() can take O(number of nodes in that level).

Across all levels, this is still:

O(n)

Therefore:

Time  = O(n)
Space = O(n)

This is already a very good solution.


Approach 2: BFS + Add to Beginning

Instead of creating the level normally and then doing:

Collections.reverse(list);

we can directly insert elements at the beginning for reverse levels.

For example:

Normal:

9 → 20

Reverse:

20 → 9

Use:

list.add(0, cur.val);

Java

class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.add(root);

        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            LinkedList<Integer> list = new LinkedList<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode cur = q.poll();

                if (leftToRight) {
                    list.addLast(cur.val);
                } else {
                    list.addFirst(cur.val);
                }

                if (cur.left != null) {
                    q.add(cur.left);
                }

                if (cur.right != null) {
                    q.add(cur.right);
                }
            }

            ans.add(list);

            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Why LinkedList?

Because:

addFirst()

is O(1) for a LinkedList.

With an ArrayList:

list.add(0, value);

can require shifting elements and may become O(n) per insertion.

Approach 3: BFS + Deque ⭐ Most Optimal / Cleanest

A Deque allows insertion from either end.

For each level:

Left → Right
addLast()
Right → Left
addFirst()

This avoids the explicit Collections.reverse().

Java

class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.add(root);

        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int size = q.size();

            Deque<Integer> level = new ArrayDeque<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode cur = q.poll();

                if (leftToRight) {
                    level.addLast(cur.val);
                } else {
                    level.addFirst(cur.val);
                }

                if (cur.left != null) {
                    q.add(cur.left);
                }

                if (cur.right != null) {
                    q.add(cur.right);
                }
            }

            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(level));

            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Important Point

Notice that we do not change the order in which children are inserted into the queue.

We always do:

if (cur.left != null)
    q.add(cur.left);

if (cur.right != null)
    q.add(cur.right);

The queue remains:

left → right

The zigzag effect is created only while building the current level.

Approach 4: DFS + Level

Another possibility is DFS.

Maintain the current level:

level = 0 → left to right
level = 1 → right to left

For even levels:

list.add(value);

For odd levels:

list.add(0, value);

Java

class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {

        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        dfs(root, 0, ans);

        return ans;
    }

    private void dfs(TreeNode root,
                     int level,
                     List<List<Integer>> ans) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (ans.size() == level) {
            ans.add(new LinkedList<>());
        }

        LinkedList<Integer> list =
            (LinkedList<Integer>) ans.get(level);

        if (level % 2 == 0) {
            list.addLast(root.val);
        } else {
            list.addFirst(root.val);
        }

        dfs(root.left, level + 1, ans);
        dfs(root.right, level + 1, ans);
    }
}

Complexity
Time  = O(n)
Space = O(h)

excluding the output.

However, for a level-order problem, BFS is more natural and easier to explain in an interview.

Comparison
Approach	Technique	Time	Extra Space
Brute Force	BFS + reverse each alternate level	O(n)	O(n)
Better	BFS + addFirst()	O(n)	O(n)
Optimal	BFS + Deque	O(n)	O(n)
Alternative	DFS + level	O(n)	O(h)

Technically, Approach 1 is already asymptotically optimal in time. The later approaches mainly improve how the alternating order is constructed.

Why size = q.size() Is Critical

This is the same important concept from LeetCode 102.

int size = q.size();

Suppose:

Queue = [9, 20]

Then:

size = 2

We process exactly two nodes.

While processing them, we may add:

15, 7

But they belong to the next level.

Therefore:

for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)

is what separates the tree into levels.

